- op: discuss  # 2f7b
  at: '2026-09-01T22:46:52Z'  # 2f7b
  by: agent  # 2f7b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2f7b
  clock: 4  # 2f7b
  nonce: 2f7b  # 2f7b
  message: "SCOPE CORRECTION, not a closure. The half of this item that motivated it --\nINV-dijor's arm-3 false positive -- shipped in PR #697 (dev 137a9474d8), but by\na DIFFERENT MECHANISM than this item's title names, so the item is re-scoped\nrather than closed.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. Arm 3 now requires the matched trailing components to AGREE IN\nCASE. sops's context.String no longer reaches gin.Context.String; 159 chains ->\n157 on the filed repo, removed 2 added 0, both removed rows read back against\nsource as urfave/cli flag reads. 1,090 exposed catalogue rows now refuse a\nvariable-spelled hint while 3,954 correctly-cased hints still match and zero\nbreak.\n\nWHY THAT IS NOT THIS ITEM. Case AGREEMENT is still INFERENCE -- a proxy for \"the\nhint names a variable rather than an owner path\" -- not the DECLARED\ndiscriminator this item asks _module_matches to read. It is a better proxy than\nwhat it replaced (it asks whether two sides are spelled the same way, which\nneeds no language, instead of whether a component is capitalised, which encodes\nGo's convention), and it was the right thing to ship first because it is\nmeasurable, language-neutral and fixes a live false positive today. But nothing\nabout it reads ADR-0051's notions.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, AND IT IS NOW THE SHARPER HALF. ARM 2 is untouched:\n\n    if longer_raw[shared][:1].isupper(): return True\n\nThat is the case-VALUE inference -- \"capitals mean types\" -- justified in its own\ndocstring by GO's naming convention while the predicate takes no language and\nserves fifteen catalogues. It is CONSTANT-TRUE, and therefore information-free,\nwherever module names are capitalised: haskell 100%, swift 97%, objc 95%, elixir\n52%, javascript 21%. Arm 3's fix does not touch this because case AGREEMENT is\nunavailable to arm 2: arm 2 compares a component that exists on ONE side only,\nso there is no second spelling to agree with. Arm 2 genuinely needs either a\nlanguage-aware policy or a declared notion -- which is exactly what this item is\nfor.\n\nFIRST STEP WHEN PICKED UP: measure arm 2 the way INV-dijor measured arm 3. Its\nharm census found the two heuristic arms decide 32 of 1,676 tagged boundaries,\nof which prefix+capital (arm 2) is 25 -- the larger share, and NOT yet\nadjudicated shape by shape the way arm 3's 7 were. Two of arm 2's matches were\nalready identified as correct BY LUCK rather than design (elixir Task <-\nTask.Supervisor and haskell Data.ByteString <- Data.ByteString.Lazy are sibling\nMODULES matched as though Supervisor/Lazy were types, which happen to share the\nboundary of the row they hit). Establish how many of the 25 are luck before\nchoosing between a per-language policy and a declared notion.\n\nSUPPLY SIDE, unchanged from this item's original note: swift.py:1223 already\nKNOWS which notion it produced (its four-clause fallback yields a type, then a\nnamespace, then a receiver variable, then a sentinel), so swift is the cheapest\nplace to prove a declared discriminator end to end. go does NOT stamp\nreceiver_type_hint, which is why WI-monul will not substitute for this."  # 2f7b
- op: discuss  # 2da7
  at: '2026-09-02T00:09:00Z'  # 2da7
  by: agent  # 2da7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2da7
  clock: 5  # 2da7
  nonce: 2da7  # 2da7
  message: "MEASURED ARM 2 END-TO-END. Not closed, and the reason is now PROVEN rather\nthan argued: no orthographic rule can fix arm 2, because the false positives\nand the true positives are ORTHOGRAPHICALLY IDENTICAL. This item's own success\ncondition (removed=0) is what the proof runs into.\n\nMETHOD. `_module_matches` replaced at runtime by an instrumented copy that\nrecords WHICH ARM decided every True, then io-boundaries run in-process over 8\nrepos chosen for the languages where the discriminator is degenerate (haskell\n195/195 catalogue rows fully capitalised, elixir 250/529, objc 126/131, swift\n106/107, javascript 39/193 -- measured over all 3,079 module-bearing rows in\n15 catalogues).\n\nARM 2 FIRES 9 TIMES ACROSS 8 REPOS, AND NAMES A TYPE **ZERO** TIMES.\nEvery one of the nine distinct (catalogue, hint) pairs is a SUB-MODULE:\n\n  Data.ByteString   <- Data.ByteString.Lazy          [postgrest, stack]\n  Data.ByteString   <- Data.ByteString.Char8         [hls, stack]\n  Data.ByteString   <- Data.ByteString.Lazy.Char8    [hls, servant, stack]\n  Data.ByteString   <- Data.ByteString.Internal      [hls]\n  System.Directory  <- System.Directory.Extra        [hls]\n  System.Process    <- System.Process.Text           [hls]\n  Req               <- Req.Response                  [livebook]\n  Task              <- Task.Supervisor               [livebook]\n  io                <- IO.ANSI                       [livebook]\n\nArm 2 fired on NO swift repo (Alamofire, Kingfisher, hummingbird: 0 each),\nwhich is worth recording because swift is 106/107 degenerate by catalogue and\nwould have been the predicted hot spot. Arm 4 (the swift carve-out) carries\nthat traffic instead.\n\nTWO CONFIRMED FALSE POSITIVES, read back against source:\n  * livebook lib/livebook/runtime/evaluator/formatter.ex:238-240\n    `defp error_color(string), do: IO.ANSI.format([:red, string], true)`\n    -- the ENTIRE body builds a chardata string and performs NO I/O.\n    Tagged `logging` via the row `io.format`. Unambiguous.\n  * livebook lib/livebook/teams/requests.ex:344-347\n    `defp add_team_auth(req, team), do: Req.Request.merge_options(req, ...)`\n    -- pure struct manipulation, no network. Tagged `net_send` via `Req.new`.\n  Plus 6 further `IO.ANSI.format` edges in livebook_cli/utils.ex whose\n  ENCLOSING function does call IO.puts, so the chain is correct by coincidence\n  while the EDGE attribution is still wrong.\n\nBUT THE HASKELL MATCHES ARE BENIGN, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE PROBLEM.\n`Data.ByteString.Lazy.readFile` really is a file read: the sibling module is a\nPARALLEL API with identical I/O semantics, so arm 2's wrong reasoning reaches\na right answer. Rejecting it would REMOVE true boundaries from\npostgrest/stack/hls/servant.\n\nTHE PROOF THAT NO HEURISTIC SUFFICES. Compare:\n    Data.ByteString + Lazy      capitalised sub-module of a capitalised module -> SHOULD match\n    Req             + Response  capitalised sub-module of a capitalised module -> SHOULD NOT\nStructurally indistinguishable. The difference is SEMANTIC (does the sibling\nre-export the same I/O surface?), which no spelling rule can see. This is\nexactly the item's thesis, now with a worked counterexample pair.\n\nTHE BEST AVAILABLE WEAKER QUESTION, and why it is still not enough. Arm 3's\nfix generalises with the polarity INVERTED: arm 3 matches the SAME components\nso they should AGREE in case; arm 2 introduces a NEW component of a different\nKIND so it should DISAGREE -- compared against the component it FOLLOWS WITHIN\nTHE LONGER PATH, never across the two paths. Checked by hand against all nine\nfirings plus the docstring's canonical cases:\n\n    os.exec      + Cmd     'exec' lower vs 'Cmd' upper   DISAGREE -> type   MATCH   (preserved)\n    net.http     + fcgi    'http' lower vs 'fcgi' lower  AGREE -> module    reject  (preserved)\n    std.fs       + File    'fs' lower vs 'File' upper    DISAGREE -> type   MATCH   (preserved)\n    java.io      + FileInputStream                       DISAGREE -> type   MATCH   (preserved)\n    Data.ByteString + Lazy 'ByteString' vs 'Lazy', both upper  AGREE -> reject\n    IO           + ANSI    'IO' vs 'ANSI', both upper    AGREE -> reject\n    Req          + Response / Task + Supervisor          AGREE -> reject\n    System.Directory + Extra / System.Process + Text     AGREE -> reject\n\nIt rejects all 9 firings and preserves every type case in the docstring. It is\nlanguage-neutral (it asks whether a component is spelled like its PARENT, not\nwhat capitals MEAN) and is strictly weaker than \"is it capitalised\".\nIT FIXES ELIXIR COMPLETELY AND BREAKS HASKELL: it removes both confirmed false\npositives at zero true-positive cost in elixir, and removes the benign haskell\nmatches, which is removed>0. Landing it would trade a false positive in one\nlanguage for a false negative in another -- the direction-flip this repo has\nalready been bitten by.\n\nTHE ONLY removed=0 ROUTE IS CATALOGUE COMPLETENESS, and it is a project, not\nan add-on: give the sibling modules their own rows so arm 1 (exact) carries\nthem and arm 2 need not fire at all. Sized: Data.ByteString is 16 rows x 3\nsiblings = 48; System.Directory.Extra (26 base rows) and System.Process.Text\n(19) are DIFFERENT PACKAGES (directory-extra, process-extras) whose APIs are\nnot identical to the base, so each row needs per-row verification under the\nstdlib dated-declaration discipline rather than a copy. ~90 rows of catalogue\nresearch.\n\nSTATUS: STILL BLOCKED, but the blocker is now identified precisely. Three ways\nforward, none of them a heuristic tweak:\n  (1) the declared per-row notion this item actually asks for -- gated behind\n      v9/v10 via WI-marok;\n  (2) the catalogue-completeness project above, then the parent-agreement rule\n      lands with removed=0;\n  (3) an owner ruling that elixir's 2 confirmed false positives outweigh\n      haskell's benign matches, permitting removed>0.\nRecording rather than choosing: (3) is an owner call and (2) is a separately\nsized piece of work."  # 2da7
